title The relation that decides, and the second package out of the box
prompt The baton -- copal seat, first lap after the fleet came back up from an account change.
think Round-open answered `already on the anointed order` and then named the dead-letter box twice: 4 session logs and 10 non-record paths standing on no ref. Both readings are my lane's. The box held TWO complete amphora packages, not one.
think The first was a whole commit parked on refs/heads/pier/diverged-20260906-210007 -- the UDP reuseaddr work, 19 files. The second was loose in stash@{0} with no session log beside it, which is exactly why `stash_record` could not see it: its subject is records, and this package wrote none.
think Rebasing the park onto main hit ONE conflict in 19 files -- the Git nib line, which every ship rewrites every lap. That is the air row's own test done for real: I pulled a part out and only the part came.
think Then the commit-msg wall refused my amend twice, and both refusals were right. The body cited `%510` as its own row and a shelf path `...rows-510.md` that is not in the tree, because the row had been renumbered to `%522` while the body was written from memory of an earlier lap. A commit body is permanent testimony; the guard caught a citation that would have been wrong forever.
think For the second package the number had to move again. Fetch-before-book against xy read next_free=514, so every `REDS %510` inside main.rye, the witness, the scan and the covers_short plant was renumbered to `%521` -- five sites, the row's own number carried in five files.
obs THE PIN WAS 193 BYTES OVER ITS BOUND BEFORE I TOUCHED IT, and the pre-commit wall refuses any commit that stages a rostered pin over bound -- and rule two stages REDS.md whenever a fold shelf is staged. So the shelf I needed to write could not land until the pin came down. `%487` and `%489` folded to one shelf, 5,796 bytes, pin 41,153 -> 35,358. They belong together: each published a TRUE verdict about a set that had emptied underneath it -- a control that ran to completion against a scratch its own trap had deleted, and a seed scan that read IDENT_CLEAN over a room that shipped zero files.
obs THE ROW ITSELF IS THE AIR ROW'S SUBJECT EXACTLY. `amphora_bounds_agree` had three readings and all three compare for EQUALITY -- same-name roofs, alias groups, declared couples -- while the two relations that decide whether Amphora refuses a pour are ORDERS. A season door welcomed a 512-character name into a listing contract holding 200, and the manifest roof was declared `= max_cargo_bytes` when the manifest is the same catalog under a three-byte-longer prefix. Both refused honestly and both refused LATE, at a wall nothing had declared, and the guard read green across the gap because no marker could express `>=`. A guard that can only say `=` is green over every gap that is a `<`.
obs THE CARD HAD THREE BYTES OF SLACK, so the COPAL block had to shrink to grow. The elder block ran 894 bytes and the honest replacement wanted 1,016; three passes brought it to 892, which is the tax the PINS directive names -- a living pin at its ceiling charges every lap a judgment call.
obs WHAT I DID NOT TAKE. Two orphan shelves in the box are superseded: `REDS-...rows-506.md` and `rows-508.md` are elder numberings of the row that landed as `%522`, and `REDS-the-reach-a-guard-never-printed-rows-493-505.md` folds two rows peers already folded to different shelves at 19:52. Dead tissue stays in the box rather than landing as a second record of one fact.
obs THE HOT PASS FOUND A RED MY OWN REBASE HAD MADE, which is the ninth firing of `%440`. Rebasing the parked commit auto-merged `session-logs/date/README-index-20260906.md`, and a prepend-shaped table has no merge that is right: main's rows and the park's rows both landed, so `20260906.173444`, `.182719` and `.190934` each stood twice and `.202036` sat below a stamp four hours older. The shelf read clean at `e567cbc128` and doubled at `17fee09702`, so the lap that found it is the lap that made it. Deduped by stamp keeping one copy and sorted descending: 113 rows -> 110, `rows_duplicate` 3 -> 0, `rows_misordered` 1 -> 0. Not booked as its own row -- `%440` already books exactly this, and a second row for one defect is the thing `%484`'s shelf refused.
obs A GUARD'S RED OUTLIVES ITS REPAIR UNTIL THE RUNNER RE-READS IT. `index_row_bound` went green under its own witness while `standing_equipment` still answered `roster_broken`, because the roster reads the RUN CARD rather than the guard, and only the runner writes that card. `sh tools/fixtures/s/standing_equipment_run.sh --hot index_row_bound` refreshed the one row in 4s, and the roster went green after it. Running a witness by hand proves the tree; it does not update the record the roster believes.
obs THE SEND'S OWN FETCH MOVED THE SPINE FIVE COMMITS AND TOOK BOTH MY NUMBERS. `xy` had gone from `e567cbc128` to `94cba0f71b` while the roster ran: a peer booked `%513` at `20260906.210117`, another `%514` at `20260906.211120`, and the pin reached `%517`. `reds_spine_derive_scan.sh` named it in one line -- `rebindings=2 squatters=2 double_booked=2 next_free=518 verdict=rebinding` -- so my two rows renumbered to `%522` and `%521` across 19 files and two shelf filenames. A published number never moves; an unshared one does, and mine were unshared because they had never reached the anointed remote. My stamps are the EARLIER ones, `154105` and `193823` against `210117` and `211120`, and the law is still right: the remote allocates, so an earlier stamp does not reclaim a number a peer already published.
obs AND MY FOLD WAS SUPERSEDED BY A BETTER ONE. I folded `%487` and `%489` together to make room on a pin 193 bytes over; while the roster ran, peers folded `%487` to its own shelf and `%489` with `%508` and `%509` to another, and booked `%517` on exactly the reading I had worked around -- a capacity scan printing `rows_that_fit=0` beside `verdict=ok`. So my shelf came out of the commit whole rather than landing as a second home for two rows already filed. Nothing was lost: their fold is the one that stands, and the pin reads 39,680.
obs THE TWO COMMITS BECAME ONE, on purpose. The renumber crossed both -- commit one's row citation lives in five files commit two never touches -- and leaving the elder commit shipping `%513` would put a peer's number into permanent testimony. Both were mine and neither had reached a remote, so a soft reset merged them and the whole package lands with the numbers it will keep.
obs A LAW LANDED UPSTREAM WHILE I WORKED, AND IT WAS ABOUT ME. `unshared_citation` arrived in the same five commits and reads every living surface for a `%N` above what the anointed spine has bound. It found THIRTEEN across TEN of my files, because I had renumbered by writing the new number everywhere. The rule is rule 4 of the derived spine -- cite by stamp until the row is shared -- and the reason is arithmetic: a `%N` is spelled in 20 to 36 files within a day, so citing an unshared number is choosing a sweep the stamp would have avoided. Every living citation is a stamp now and the guard reads zero.
obs AND THE SCAN'S OWN HEADER DESCRIBED THE MISTAKE I HAD JUST MADE. It cites a renumber that "ran as a blind `sed` that rewrote a peer's row and two upstream passages" -- and mine rewrote a peer's row in `construction/standing-equipment.kyri` and two rows of the day shelf, one Bakery's `%510` and one a peer's `%513, %514`. Caught by diffing every substitution against the pre-sed tree rather than by trusting the sed's file list; restored from upstream's own text. A blanket substitution over a shared file cannot tell my number from yours, and the file list is not the safeguard -- the diff is.
obs ONE RED RIDES IN AND I AM NOT REPAIRING IT. `construction/CHECKPOINTS.md`'s `20260906.210719` row links `archive/REDS-a-destructive-act-aimed-by-a-name-rows-487-512.md`, a shelf that exists in no tree including the one that wrote it: the fold that checkpoint preceded split three ways under three other names. It reds `readme_reach`, cascades into `reds_fold` (whose first assert is that the reach scan runs), and through those into `standing_equipment` -- three guards, one root, every tree in the fleet. I am leaving it named rather than repointed: the checkpoint ledger is append-only by its own law, the three candidate shelves each hold a different row set, and a link repointed to the wrong shelf OPENS and never reds, which is precisely the defect this seat closed this morning.
obs AND THEN IT HAPPENED A THIRD TIME, WHICH IS THE MEASUREMENT. The fetch before the push moved `xy` again and peers had booked `%518` at `20260906.221454` and `%519` at `20260906.210016`, so the two rows became `%522` and `%521`. Counting from their first booking these two numbers have moved ELEVEN times in one day while their two stamps -- `20260906.154105` and `20260906.193823` -- have not moved once. That is the card's standing question answered with a price rather than an opinion: a number is a view allocated by a remote, a stamp is the row's identity, and the only reason this third renumber was cheap is that the living surfaces had already been converted to stamps an hour earlier. The first renumber touched 19 files; this one touched 10, and 2 of those were filenames.
obs THE INHERITED RED CLOSED ITSELF WHILE I WORKED. `CHECKPOINTS.md`'s broken shelf link had `readme_reach` at `broken_in_living=2`, then 1, and after this rebase `verdict=ok` at 0 -- its author repaired it upstream. The card block naming it was rewritten rather than shipped stale, which is the whole reason a lap re-reads its own findings after the last fetch instead of before it.
obs THE COLD PASS FOUND TWO MORE REDS AND NEITHER WAS MINE, YET BOTH HAD EXACTLY ONE ANSWER. A peer folded a block out of the card into `construction/archive/20260906-222720_itinerary-landed-accounts.md` and carried its `archive/`-prefixed link down with it, so the link now resolves one directory too deep; and `REDS-the-name-asked-one-question-rows-516-517.md` line 19 wears the anchor `%510` over a path naming `%511`'s shelf, while its own sentence says *a guard that read a proxy* -- which is `%510`. That is the class my `%502` booked this morning, found in someone else's file by the guard that row landed. Both are the fold-reanchor step `reds_fold.sh`'s own header calls *the one a hand forgets*, and both repairs are computed rather than chosen, so I made them: `fold_depth_lost` 1 -> 0 and `citation_disagrees` -> `verdict=ok`. Where the earlier CHECKPOINTS break had three candidate shelves and I left it alone, these have one apiece, and that difference is the whole test for whether a peer's file is mine to touch.
